From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 12 Mar 94 12:07:27 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-147] PCR

Tim Starr replies to Eric Forste:
>>You and Tim Starr seem to be interested in understanding PCR and
>>evolutionary epistemology. I'm interested in discovering whether and how
>>your absolutist epistemological ideas are compatible with Extropian ideas
>>in general.
>
>"Absolutism"?  What does the divine right of kings have to do with it?

I've been checking out references to "absolutism", and true, absolute
authoritarianism / divine right of kings is one obvious meaning.  In
philosophy, though, there are: (i) Hegel's ideal and evolving nature of
ultimate reality, which he called the "Absolute".  (ii) The assertion of
the existence and authority of absolute truth.

Taking the latter definition, I suspect we all believe in this to at least
some degree, e.g. "I think" and "I am".  The main dispute is over the
knowledge / belief dividing line:

absolutists /      {  select 
justificationists /   preferred
naive empiricists /     term 
Cartesian rationalists / to
objectivists /          taste   
Objectivists       }                           evolut'ry epistemologists
etc.                                              /     PCRists
------------------------                       ------------------------
certainties      beliefs                       certainties      beliefs
-----------      -------                       -----------      -------  

existence        Deng                          I am             existence                   
exists           Xiaoping                                       exists
                 was alive                     I think 
I am             12 hours                                       A is A
                 ago 
A is A                                                          Deng
                                                                Xiaoping
I think                                                         was alive
                                                                12 hours
                                                                ago


So accepting Reilly Jones' point about certainty's digital nature, the 
PCRist would probably be better off saying: "some of your [absolutists']
'certainties' are only truly certainties with respect to your subjective
reality; they are mere beliefs as to the nature of objective reality. Some
'certainties' are relative, but these are the absolutists' 'certainties',
not the PCRists' certainties.  Therefore everyone is certain that certainty
is not relative, according to their own definitions.  But the class of
things that are 'certainties' varies from one person to the next, so the
boundary of certainty is relative.  And everyone is certain that belief is
relative."

Some day language might be so advanced that fallacies of ambiguity could
never arise, but this could render discussions rather sterile.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

