From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 12 Mar 94 18:37:57 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-148] LANG: Evolution of language (was ANTHRO: Mulitple origins)

Simon! Levy writes:
>Language clearly evolved at some point in the distant past, but it makes more
>sense to say that, since that time, language hasn't evolved, but rather changed.

This brings up an interesting point that I'd like to be clear on -- how to
tell mere changes from evolution.  And a suggested distinction:

>The changes are not directed toward some end state; in fact, they are more 
>likely cyclic, or like movement through some multi-dimensional state space.

The great thing about biological evolution is that you can start with 
primitive, simple objects, and end up with advanced, complex, conscious
lifeforms.  It seems as if the changes are directed towards an end state
-- production of the complex lifeforms.  Now if evolution has to be
advancement towards some end state, then viruses haven't evolved since
Pre-Cambrian times, and insects stopped evolving in the Carboniferous
period.  (Although it might not always be possible to be entirely
objective about whether a change is an advancement, a stagnation, or a
decline.)  If we accept this definition, language could still be
considered to be evolving (albeit very slowly), but only if you expect it
to be more advanced in the future than it now is.  

Evolution has been defined as the process by which primordial life has
developed by successive stages into the multiplicity of animal and plant
life, extinct and existing.  So I'd class any similar process of
inaccurate copying as evolution.  One natural consequence of such 
evolution is that given sufficient copying and variation, each niche gets
filled, and the fact that sometimes an "end-product" seems to arise is
just another natural outcome.  So in the evolution of language, each niche
gradually gets filled by a word or phrase.  Sometimes random mutations
occur, at other times new concepts are invented (or discovered), and a new
niche arises to be filled by a neologism.  And as time passes the language
gets more sophisticated, moving towards the end state -- perfection.

In *The Selfish Gene*, Dawkins seems quite happy with the idea of memetic
evolution.  Memes are mental structures which make copies of themselves,
sometimes inaccurately but sometimes by design, and the process isn't
disqualified as evolution just because it's fairly Lamarckian.  Some memes
will have a higher survival value than others, and the same thing could be
said about words (cf. apodictic vs. a).  Language requires the same sort
of brain to brain propagation as does memetic evolution -- it might be
considered a subdivision of memetic evolution, in fact.  A meme or meme-
complex could represent a tune, symphony, design of a new product, new
scientific theory, or catchphrase, so why not a single word or a vocabulary?

Design of an artificial language could not be evolution, but even then as
mentioned in Simon!'s *Extropy* article, the artificial language could
mutate into a natural language.  In the free market for languages,
competition between them would lead to one being the more popular at any
given time -- a sort of end-product, or "winner".

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

