From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sun, 13 Mar 94 01:15:04 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-152] Natural Rights and Ancient Liberties

Tim Starr says:
>>...I suspect there's a problem with
>>externalities -- e.g. if I own a smoky factory do I have the right to
>>pollute the air, or if an oil tanker do I have the right to unleash a
>>slick?
>
>The principle of pollution as tresspass clarifies this.  It all comes
>down to who owns the air or water.  No one has the right to pollute
>the property of another.

This principle settles some cases: I have the right to pollute my airspace
but only if there is no discernible pollution to another person's air.
But what about the airspace over public roads -- is every motorist
technically guilty of trespass by polluting this air, ditto for every
horse rider who doesn't clear up and disinfect any mess left?  And what
about oil slicks polluting public beaches?

Universality (all rational beings) seems to leave scope for individual
interpretation -- do fetuses / babies / children / backward people count
as rational, and could future AIs ever be classed as rational?  (Sorry,
that came to 8 questions!)  Statists could claim that we must be
irrational since we don't support statism; hence we have no rights. 

>>(i)  When humans are free to pursue their own chosen goals, they are led
>>by "an invisible hand", a spontaneous order, to promote unintended ends,
>>which are frequently in the best interests of human society.
>>
>>(ii)  [longer description of natural rights deleted]
>>
>>It looks like (ii) is just a more longwinded way of saying (i), which is
>>already known by supporters of free markets.
>
>(ii) is more concrete than (i).  That's helpful to those who aren't very
>good at abstraction.

Interpretation pluralism has advantages for outreach purposes; "rights"
are less abstract than "invisible hands", whereas some, like many
Extropians, tend to prefer the more abstract description.  Here's a
compromise version that I like: 

(iii)  There are certain rights and liberties inherent in the nature of
man, which when acquired will unleash a powerhouse of constructive
activities, thereby significantly increasing general prosperity.  These
rights apply to everybody, and are derived from the time-honored right
not to be interfered with. 

But if natural rights theory is just another way of stating a section of
Austrian economics that would be readily accepted by libertarians and
anarchists, why isn't it accepted as virtually axiomatic by Extropians?

*  There was the idea of "rights written in the sky", which I don't think
natural rights (of humans) advocates ever claimed.  There are no
metaphysical "rights".

*  Lexical ambiguity arising from "rights" (21 numbered definitions in
my dictionary for "right", can any other word beat it?).  All but two can
be eliminated:  n. 14. claim, title etc allowed or due  15. what is right,
just or due.  "Animal rights" implies (15) and an absolute morality.
Morality is like space and time: you can consider the dimension whose axis
most closely matches the average background motion of matter to be the
cosmological time axis.  But this cannot be determined accurately enough
to provide an absolute time with which all observers would agree.  There
is a similar Schelling point in ethics: many people will roughly agree on
the boundaries of what is wrong, but there can never be sufficient
agreement to make it absolute.  However, I think natural rights (of
rational beings) people only used the "claim allowed" version of rights.

*  Then we have the supposed objectivity of these natural rights, but the
trouble is, it's like absolute space and time, and absolute morality.
The boundary between who has rights and who doesn't may be approximately
agreed, but the distinction is too blurred to produce an objective
definition that would always be agreed upon by all observers.  But the
thing is, it doesn't matter.

Hayek supports the use of methodological pluralism, although by 1967 he
acknowledged that the gap between the natural and social sciences was not
as great as he'd previously assumed.  Slavishly applying the methods of
the natural sciences to the social sciences is described as 'scientistic',
in other words -- unscientific.  Now this does lead to an admission that
since the 'facts' of the social sciences are mostly subjective and not
susceptible to the objective methods of the natural sciences, an
objectivity of knowledge comparable to that of the natural sciences can
clearly not be attained.  But to strive for this objectivity would go
against the principle of methodological pluralism and would be
unscientific, according to Hayek.  

So I start with the "invisible hand" description as the preferred choice
due to Occam's Razor, my (iii) variation is an acceptable alternative,
and other natural rights variations would probably do for spreading the
ideas to certain people, provided it's not claimed that these rights
have the objectivity that the sums of any two three-digit numbers have,
for instance.  The same thing might be said about goals.  Although if
someone claims that rights are just a convenient fiction which we use to
help achieve our goals, to say that goals are a convenient fiction which
help to enforce our rights doesn't sound quite right.

>>You gave the example of the Dutch becoming prosperous after enforcing 
>>natural rights, which could possibly be used as evidence.  If human nature
>>may be such that a set of natural rights exist which promote prosperity if
>>enforced or respected, how could this be falsified if rights were never
>>enforced or respected?  If I claimed that a genie existed who could
>>dramatically increase human prosperity if he wanted to, critics would
>>remark: "How convenient it is for the theory that he never chooses to!"
>
>I see: there's a methodological difference here.  I would argue that the
>fact that rights-enforcement leads to increased prosperity in pareto-
>optimal fashion can be deduced from praxaeological axioms plus a few
>explicit empirical assumptions, even if there had never been a society
>in which rights had been enforced.  This would be unfalsifiable, but I
>don't think the requirement of falsifiability applies here.

We might be able to pick some axioms and find that increased prosperity
is an almost certain conclusion, but what about socialists and statists?
They aren't going to be convinced by a lack of empirical evidence (they
normally aren't convinced by evidence either).  I'd like to know just
where socialists would differ -- it seems that they'd reject the axiom of
methodological individualism, for instance, instead opting for what Popper
calls "the totalitarian intuition".  The holists / constructivists attempt
to study 'society' by an impossible method, and also try to control and
reconstruct society as a whole.  In my view, the holistic method fails
because the individual human brain is the only local entity capable of
rational action; by comparison states, economies, ecosystems, etc are
entropic, irrational giants.  Purpose originates from the more complex,
concrete, extropy-generating objects, rather than from abstract, chaotic,
disconnected, postulated wholes.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

