From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>
Date: Sat, 12 Mar 1994 22:34:52 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-154] Is Certainty Relative?

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>  On  Fri, 11 Mar 1994 wrote:
	
		 > As far as I'm  concerned, certainty is roughly the same as
		 >knowledge. One is certain of  the things one knows, and one        
		 >knows the things one is certain of. 
		 

Certainty is not the same as knowledge. If I say I am certain of
a proposition it tells you only that my mental state is such
that I have no doubt that it is true but it tells you nothing
about whether it is in fact true. I can't dispute the
proposition (it's indisputable) but this could be due to lack of
cleverness on my part or lack of a vital peace of information.
		 
There are two kinds of knowledge "knowledge how " ( riding a
bicycle ), and "knowledge that "(water is made of hydrogen and
oxygen) ; were talking about the "knowledge that" kind. If I
believe in a true thing for the wrong reason ( I "know" that  2
X 8 = 16 because I know that 2 X 2 = 8  and  2 X 2 X 2 = 16 so 2
X 8 = 16) that is not knowledge. About the most rigorous
definition of knowledge is justified true belief but even this
is too ambiguous for Philosophy.
		 
I heard a story about this, it may be apocryphal . On November 7
1918 several newspapers in Boston ran a erroneous story that the
war had ended . On the same day two men set sail in a small
boat. They arrived in Bermuda on November 11 1918 certain that
the war was over. They had perfectly reasonable evidence ,
copies of the newspapers, and there belief was even true ( the
war ended on Nov. 11) but it was not knowledge.
		 
This is not the only trouble with knowledge. Knowledge implies
certainty ( but certainty does not imply knowledge). How firmly
must I hold a true belief for it to be knowledge? Certainty is
absolute, either I have doubts or I don't but knowledge is a
matter of degrees. One could reserve the word knowledge for
justified true beliefs that one is absolutely certain of but
this is not how the word is used in common usage and common
usage is all it's good for, the word is just too vague and
ambiguous for Philosophy. It's much more precise to talk about
if a belief is true or not and how firmly it is held; of this I
am certain. 
		 
			     John K Clark               johnkc@well.sf.ca.us

