Subject: [#94-3-155] LANG: Evolution of language

Nan Wolfslayer asks:

> -- I thought of Jung when I was writing this post.  Does he believe that
> humans have a kind of pre-existent 'hardware' like Chomsky's idea on the
> basis of language as something 'built in' (is this right Simon!?)  Anyway,

Your understanding of Chomsky seems to agree with mine; i.e., that he views
the idiosyncratic properties of human language as derving from "innate
rules" (his term) that are part of our "language organ" (ditto).  I haven't
read much Jung.
 
>---Do we evolve within language or does language evolve within us?

Language, like clothing fashions, is behavior. I recently attended a talk by 
William Labov, the founder of the discipline of sociolinguistics, entitled 
"Is Sound Change Adaptive?"  The answer, to no one's surprise, was no.
(Labov was pulling the old trick of providing a provocative but irrelevant
title to introduce his latest work -- which is fascinating for other reaons.)
So, if you don't believe that fashions evolve (and I see no reason to 
believe that they do), you probably shouldn't believe that language
evolves, in the usual Darwinian adaptive sense of the term.

> --humm, good point.  I'll have to look up the difference between evolution
> and change.  More later...

Evolution is the process by which mostly random genetic changes (mutations) 
spread in a population because of the advantage produced by the change.
There has been some fascinating work done (mostly by phoneticians, who
generally don't buy into the Chomsky dogma) showing that certain types
of speech sounds are likelier to occur than others, based on the 
reletive communicative efficiency of the sounds.  But it is essential to
keep in mind that the "supporting meidum" of language -- human beings with
brains and vocal tracts -- is qualitatively different from the supporting
medium of evolution -- genes and reproducing populations -- when you compare
the two systems.

>>The changes are not directed toward some end state; in fact, they are more 
>>likely cyclic, or like movement through some multi-dimensional state space.

>I think that this is a really neat idea.  Do you know anything about the
>Penrose Transform Theory in Mathamatics?  

Nope.  I was thinking more along the lines of attractors and repellors in
a dynamical system, like the ones popularized by the Santa Cruz "Chaos
Collective" (Abraham, Shaw, Farmer, et al) in the Eighties.  

> -- Can metaphor be used to extend semantic categories?  I really, really,
> really want to believe that and did;  until I read Donald Davidson's
> article of Metaphor and Meaning.  I was kind of hoping that you would have
> read Davidson and could help me out with a brilliant argument against his
> theis that all meanings in metaphor are literal.  

I don't recall ever reading Davidson.  By "extending semantic categories"
I was referring to the tendency of languages to extend formal categories
(e.g., noun classes) by metaphor.  Lakoff, citing Dixon, gives the example
of Dyirbal, in which women and birds are part of the same noun class.
Apparently, birds are considered to be the spirits of dead human females
in Dyribal speakers' mythology (cf. Yeats: "A woman's beauty is like a white
frail bird", my all-time favorite metaphor), which accounts for the
grouping-together of woman and birds in that noun class.  All languages
do this to some extent, even English, where we find people referring
to small objects as "puppies" or "bad boys" (esp. common in the speech
of twentysomething men).  Languages differ in the extent to which the
class-words are morphologized (i.e., made are required part of the grammar).
For example, in Japanese, you can't just say "Give me five pencils"; you have
to say (literally) "Give me five-<cylindrical-noun-classifier> pencils".  

>> The best-
>>known researcher in that last area is George Lakoff, author of _Women, Fire,
>>and Dangerous Things_, and _Metaphors We Live By_. I would recommend the
>>latter,
>>as the former tends to be overly technical at many points.  

> --I've seen this book at Cody's but was offended by the title so I didn't
> bother to pick it up.  I'll go look at it some more.  On another point,
> Lakoff has quite a rep here at Berkeley for being bossy and rude as well as
