>
>This principle settles some cases: I have the right to pollute my airspace
>but only if there is no discernible pollution to another person's air.
>But what about the airspace over public roads -- is every motorist
>technically guilty of trespass by polluting this air, ditto for every
>horse rider who doesn't clear up and disinfect any mess left?  And what
>about oil slicks polluting public beaches?

The principle only applies to private property.  Public property is right
out, so the way to implement it is to maximally privatize property.  For
property that can't be privatized, the best you can do is get accountability
through just compensation to victims for damages done.  In the case of car
pollution, since there's no proof (yet) that it's a significant public health
risk, it would be permitted.

>Universality (all rational beings) seems to leave scope for individual
>interpretation -- do fetuses / babies / children / backward people count
>as rational, and could future AIs ever be classed as rational?  (Sorry,
>that came to 8 questions!)  Statists could claim that we must be
>irrational since we don't support statism; hence we have no rights. 

By "rational," I mean: in possession of rational faculties, having the capacity
of reason, not "exercising this faculty at the present time."  This leaves
borderline cases such as fetuses, babies, young people, old people, and
cognitively handicapped people, but we're talking about a presumption here.
Presuming them rational makes sure to include as many as possible, and
leaving this presumption to be overcome by sufficient demonstration - as
determined by some judiciary hearing such as trial by jury - leaves room to
exclude those who can be proven not rational.

>(iii)  There are certain rights and liberties inherent in the nature of
>man, which when acquired will unleash a powerhouse of constructive
>activities, thereby significantly increasing general prosperity.  These
>rights apply to everybody, and are derived from the time-honored right
>not to be interfered with. 

I'd still prefer "enforced" to "acquired."

>But if natural rights theory is just another way of stating a section of
>Austrian economics that would be readily accepted by libertarians and
>anarchists, why isn't it accepted as virtually axiomatic by Extropians?
>
>*  There was the idea of "rights written in the sky", which I don't think
>natural rights (of humans) advocates ever claimed.  There are no
>metaphysical "rights".

This does seem to be a strawman.

>*  Lexical ambiguity arising from "rights" (21 numbered definitions in
>my dictionary for "right", can any other word beat it?).

Try "nice" in the OED.  Saw a TV show that said it had the most entries.

>We might be able to pick some axioms and find that increased prosperity
>is an almost certain conclusion, but what about socialists and statists?
>...it seems that they'd reject the axiom of
>methodological individualism...
>...[their method] fails
>because the individual human brain is the only local entity capable of
>rational action; by comparison states, economies, ecosystems, etc are
>entropic, irrational giants.  Purpose originates from the more complex,
>concrete, extropy-generating objects, rather than from abstract, chaotic,
>disconnected, postulated wholes.

What about statists?  How are they different?  They're methodological
monists, for one thing, and their method is reductionism.  Thus, in order
to study "society," they start with groups of people and try to reduce them
to their parts.  The Hayekian alternative is the "compositive" method, by
which the study of society starts with individual people, from which
social processes are "composed" by extrapolation.

However, I think it's does disservice to social processes to describe them
as "entropic" and chaotic.  Rather, I'd say that their order is one that
can only be arrived at compositively, rather than reductively, or at least
that the compositive method is a necessary condition to the understanding
of social order.  Reductionism alone won't do.

You're quite right that purpose originates from individual actors, but there's
more to purpose in social processes.  There's also function, which needn't be
purposive - this is what the unintended consequences of intended actions is
all about, as in public choice theory.  States, etc., are incapable of
purposeful behavior, but they are capable of predictable behavior.

