Subject: [#94-3-161] Singularity Aggravations

I believe that after the Singularity, nothing will quite fit
anything else--it will be like dealing with computers, except that
it won't just be computers, it will also be your mind, your body,
your desktop factory and your society.

While the rewards for making various systems compatible will be
large, there will be lots of people operating at the limits of
their expanded intelligences inventing new things that won't fit--
and even if they're careful about compatibility (a great risk,
since it may mean getting their product to the market too late),
other people will be designing things at the same time that won't
be compatible.

I don't think that this will lead to a diaster--it might even
be more robust than a high compatibility world. (There's no
virus that could infect all computers now.) On the other hand,
it's going to be a pain--you'll live for millenia (at least)
at an accelerating rate that will make it seem even longer--
and you'll spend at least a third of it trying to get things
to work.  

Nancy Lebovitz

